 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Cache Replacement (2Q)
+
+The cache maintains two queues of cache entries, both ordered by use and evicted from their oldest end:
+
+- **A1 — first-use queue** (FIFO, 4 GiB). Every newly completed sheet enters A1 at its most-recently-used end.
+- **A2 — reused queue** (LRU, holds the remainder of the 12 GiB budget, 8 GiB initially). Only sheets that were hit again while in A1 enter A2.
+
+On a cache hit:
+
+- A hit in A1 promotes the entry to the most-recently-used end of A2. Promotion moves queue metadata only; the JPEG file does not move.
+- A hit in A2 refreshes the entry to the most-recently-used end of A2.
+
+A long sequential screening fills and cycles A1 without displacing the reused comparison sheets in A2.
+
+On admission of a completed sheet, place it at the most-recently-used end of A1, then evict until the cache is within 12 GiB and each queue is within its own bound. Evict oldest A1 entries first; evict A2 least-recently-used entries only once A1 is within its own bound and the total still exceeds the budget. Eviction removes the entry and its JPEG file together.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache replacement policy | 2Q with an A1/A2 queue split | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Long sequential screenings would evict repeatedly reused comparison sheets under plain LRU. Aged frequency counters add per-hit maintenance and retain stale favorites after editors switch projects. 2Q separates first-use sheets from reused ones, protecting the comparison working set at the cost of two queues. The change is confined to the preview worker's private cache; the interface, render queue, and disk budget are untouched. |
+| Ghost list for evicted A1 keys | Omitted | 2Q variant with an A1-out ghost-key list | A ghost list reduces re-render churn after eviction, but at roughly 450 ms per sheet re-rendering an evicted sheet is acceptable. Omitting ghosts keeps the bookkeeping to two queues. |
+| Queue-size split | Byte bounds: A1 = 4 GiB, A2 = the remainder (8 GiB initially) | Splitting by entry count; equal byte split | Byte bounds compose directly with the 12 GiB admission rule. 4 GiB of A1 absorbs a typical screening wave (about 2,000 two-MiB sheets) while leaving roughly 8 GiB for reused sheets. Exact split to be tuned during the pilot. |
+
